---
import Slide from "../../components/Slide.astro"
import CodeBlock from "../../components/CodeBlock.astro"
// import { Image } from 'astro:assets'
---
<Slide t1="Moving Things Around">
    <ul>
        <li class="fragment">
            We can move objects around by calling <code>transform.Translate</code>
                with a vector (x, y, z)
        </li>

        <li class="fragment">
            This is a fixed movement <em>per frame</em>&mdash;it isn't guaranteed
            to be a fixed speed
        </li>
    </ul>
</Slide>

<Slide t2="Starter Script">
    <p>Kick off with a brand new project.</p>
    <div class="text-sm-1">
        <CodeBlock lang="csharp" height="350px">
            { `
public class Mover: MonoBehaviour
{
    public float xSpeed = 0;

    ...

    void Update()
    {
        Vector3 v = new Vector3(xSpeed, 0, 0);

        transform.Translate(v);
        ...
    }
}
            `.trim() }
        </CodeBlock>
    </div>
</Slide>

<Slide t2="Simple Exercises">
    <ol>
        <li class="fragment">Play with the editor: change the speed and direction</li>
        <li class="fragment">How about the other directions: vertical, front-back?</li>
    </ol>
</Slide>

<Slide t2="Delta Time">
    <ul>
        <li>To get a constant speed, regardless of frame rate,
            multiply by <code>Time.deltaTime</code>
            (the fractional time between frames)</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="text-sm-1">
        <CodeBlock lang="csharp" height="150px">
            { `
Vector3 v = new Vector3(xSpeed, 0, 0) * Time.deltaTime;

transform.Translate(v);
            `.trim() }
        </CodeBlock>
    </div>
</Slide>

<Slide t2="Reset Example">
    <ul>
        <li>When the sphere moves too far to the right, bring it back to the left</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="text-sm-1">
        <CodeBlock lang="csharp" height="150px">
            { `
if (transform.position.x > 10) {
    transform.position = new Vector3(-10, 0, 0);
}
            `.trim() }
        </CodeBlock>
    </div>

    <ul>
        <li class="fragment">Exercise: make the limit (<code>10</code>)
            into a variable we can alter inside Unity</li>
        <li class="fragment">Exercise: add limits for <code>y</code>
            and <code>z</code></li>
    </ul>

</Slide>

<Slide t2="Affecting Colour">
    <ul class="text-sm-1">
        <li class="fragment">We have already seem how to alter object colours in code</li>
        <li class="fragment">We can now do this based on position, speed, ...</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="fragment text-sm-1">
        <CodeBlock lang="csharp" height="375px">
            { `
private Renderer itsRenderer;

void Start()
{
    itsRenderer = GetComponent<Renderer>();
}

void Update()
{
    if (...) {
        itsRenderer.material.color = Color.black;
    } else {
        itsRenderer.material.color = Color.white;
    }
}
            `.trim() }
        </CodeBlock>
    </div>
</Slide>

<Slide t2="Bounce Example">
    <ul>
        <li class="fragment">Similar to the reset example: make the
            sphere "bounce" between two limits (rather than jumping from
            one to the other)
        </li>

        <li class="fragment">Exercise: make the sphere change colour
            (between black and white) every time it bounces
        </li>
    </ul>
</Slide>

<Slide t2="Now In Colour">
    <ul class="text-sm-1">
        <li class="fragment">We can create RGB colours using the
            <code>Color()</code> constructor rather than hard-wired
            names
        </li>
    </ul>

    <div class="fragment text-sm-1">
        <CodeBlock lang="csharp" height=50px">
            { `
itsRenderer.material.color = new Color(1.0, 0.7, 0.0)
            `.trim() }
        </CodeBlock>
    </div>

    <ul class="text-sm-1">
        <li class="fragment">
            Modify the project to generate an RGB mix: red depends on
            <code>x</code> direction, green on <code>y</code>, blue on <code>z</code>
        </li>

        <li class="fragment">
            Variant: colour mix depends on position, not speed
        </li>

        <li class="fragment">
            Bonus: add toggle controls (tick boxes) to the Unity UI
            to switch between them
        </li>
    </ul>
</Slide>
